    "information": "### Page note: https://d2iwpl8k086uu2.cloudfront.net/docs/default-source/s3-library/publication-pdfs/ci-alto-mayo-rap-73-report.pdf\nStored documents: doc-3bcc3ab978dd\nReader state: complete; relevance: partial\n**Page:** PDF document *Evaluación Biológica Rápida del Paisaje Alto Mayo, San Martín, Perú* (RAP Bulletin 73, 2024) – https://d2iwpl8k086uu2.cloudfront.net/docs/default-source/s3-library/publication-pdfs/ci-alto-mayo-rap-73-report.pdf  \n\n**Evidence**  \n- **Table 1 – Summary of major results**  \n  - Bats / Murciélagos: 62 total species, 28 species newly recorded in Alto Mayo, 1 species new to science, 1 species potentially new to science.  \n    *(Source excerpt – Table 1: “Bats / Murciélagos 62 28 1 1”)*  \n- Mention that **at least 28 bat species have been newly recorded**, with 1 new to science and 1 potentially new to science, but no individual species names are listed in the extracted text.  \n- The document refers to **Chapter 9 “Murciélagos del paisaje Alto Mayo”** which presumably contains the full species list, but the chapter contents are not present in the excerpt.\n\n**Missing**  \n- Comprehensive list of the species in the subfamily *Phyllostominae* documented during the 2022 RAP expedition.  \n- Names of species classified as new to science / potentially new to science within the subfamily.  \n- Any specific distribution or measurement data for those species.\n\n**Next links**  \n- The PDF’s Table of Contents lists Chapter 9, “Murciélagos del paisaje Alto Mayo”, which likely contains the detailed list of bat species, including those in the subfamily *Phyllostominae*.  \n- A “Report at a Glance” page and the “Table 1” provide aggregate counts but not individual species.",
    "information_chars": 1705,
    "own_information": "**Page:** PDF document *Evaluación Biológica Rápida del Paisaje Alto Mayo, San Martín, Perú* (RAP Bulletin 73, 2024) – https://d2iwpl8k086uu2.cloudfront.net/docs/default-source/s3-library/publication-pdfs/ci-alto-mayo-rap-73-report.pdf  \n\n**Evidence**  \n- **Table 1 – Summary of major results**  \n  - Bats / Murciélagos: 62 total species, 28 species newly recorded in Alto Mayo, 1 species new to science, 1 species potentially new to science.  \n    *(Source excerpt – Table 1: “Bats / Murciélagos 62 28 1 1”)*  \n- Mention that **at least 28 bat species have been newly recorded**, with 1 new to science and 1 potentially new to science, but no individual species names are listed in the extracted text.  \n- The document refers to **Chapter 9 “Murciélagos del paisaje Alto Mayo”** which presumably contains the full species list, but the chapter contents are not present in the excerpt.\n\n**Missing**  \n- Comprehensive list of the species in the subfamily *Phyllostominae* documented during the 2022 RAP expedition.  \n- Names of species classified as new to science / potentially new to science within the subfamily.  \n- Any specific distribution or measurement data for those species.\n\n**Next links**  \n- The PDF’s Table of Contents lists Chapter 9, “Murciélagos del paisaje Alto Mayo”, which likely contains the detailed list of bat species, including those in the subfamily *Phyllostominae*.  \n- A “Report at a Glance” page and the “Table 1” provide aggregate counts but not individual species.",
